diagnosis_text:
I cannot reproduce the “two islands” condition; end-to-end reachability across the “old” and “new” halves is working. From pc0 (20.1.1.10) to pc12 (120.1.1.10) and back, topology-wide reachability shows 0% loss and sub‑millisecond RTTs both directions. Additionally, a targeted probe from pc0 to the new-side gateway r12 (120.1.1.1) succeeds with 0% loss, confirming forwarding through the middle of the network.

Control-plane state also indicates the core is stitched together: central routers (e.g., r1 and r3) have large OSPF-learned tables for opposite-side prefixes, and the new-side edge (r11/r12/r13) shows EIGRP reachability for its local 102/112/113/120 networks with active redistribution to the rest of the fabric. r3 advertises and learns across multiple segments, and r1 sees remote networks via r3, demonstrating no OSPF area partition. The presence of consistent OSPF/EIGRP routes across devices and the successful host pings argue against a current split-brain.

Root cause: Not currently present; likely a transient control-plane flap during last night’s maintenance that has since converged.

Corrective fix: None required now. The fabric is healthy and both halves are “married” again. As a precaution, monitor OSPF/EIGRP neighbor logs on r3/r11/r12 for flaps and confirm maintenance-induced changes (timers, redistribution) match standards to avoid future partitions.
=== END ===